Supreme People's Court

Decision on Several Issues Concerning the Establishment of Maritime Courts

(November 28, 1984)

To the High People's Courts of all provinces, autonomous regions, and municipalities directly under the Central Government; the Military Court of the People's Liberation Army; and the Railway Transport High Court:

The "Decision of the Standing Committee of the National People's Congress on the Establishment of Maritime Courts in Coastal Port Cities," adopted at the Eighth Meeting of the Standing Committee of the Sixth National People's Congress, has been promulgated and put into effect by the order of Li Xiannian, President of the People's Republic of China. Accordingly, the following decision is made on several issues concerning the establishment of maritime courts:

I. Maritime courts shall be established in the following port cities:

1. Establish the Guangzhou Maritime Court in Guangzhou City;
2. Establish the Shanghai Maritime Court in Shanghai City;
3. Establish the Qingdao Maritime Court in Qingdao City;
4. Establish the Tianjin Maritime Court in Tianjin City;
5. Establish the Dalian Maritime Court in Dalian City.

II. Each maritime court shall have the following organs: a Maritime Trial Division, an Admiralty Trial Division, a Research Office, and a General Office. The establishment of organs and the allocation of personnel shall both strive for simplicity and efficiency.

III. The scope of cases accepted by the maritime courts is tentatively defined as the following maritime and admiralty cases that shall be under the jurisdiction of our country according to law, whether between domestic enterprises, organizations, or citizens; between Chinese enterprises, organizations, or citizens and foreign enterprises, organizations, or citizens; or between foreign enterprises, organizations, or citizens:

1. Cases concerning compensation for damages arising from collisions between vessels;
2. Cases concerning compensation for damages arising from collisions between vessels and offshore or port facilities;
3. Cases concerning compensation for damages arising from water pollution caused by the discharge of harmful substances from vessels or improper measures in maritime operations;
4. Cases concerning claims for economic losses caused by offshore operation facilities affecting vessel navigation;
5. Cases concerning claims for compensation for damages arising from personal injury or death occurring during maritime transport and offshore or port operations;
6. Cases concerning serious liability accidents in maritime transport and offshore operations (if during the trial it is discovered that a crime has been committed requiring criminal liability to be pursued, the case shall be transferred to the public security and procuratorial authorities for handling in accordance with the procedures prescribed by law);
7. Cases concerning disputes over contracts for the carriage of goods by sea;
8. Cases concerning disputes over contracts for the carriage of passengers and baggage by sea;
9. Cases concerning disputes over contracts for the charter, agency, or repair of vessels;
10. Cases concerning disputes over marine insurance operations;
11. Cases concerning disputes over salvage, wreck removal, and towage at sea;
12. Cases concerning disputes over general average;
13. Cases concerning disputes over port loading, unloading, and tallying operations;
14. Cases concerning disputes over marine development and utilization;
15. Cases where, prior to filing a lawsuit, one party applies for the adoption of preservation measures to arrest a vessel due to maritime or admiralty disputes;
16. Cases where a party, having been penalized by the competent administrative authority for violating relevant maritime laws or regulations, files a lawsuit within the statutory time limit; or cases where the party neither files a lawsuit within the time limit nor complies upon expiration, and the competent administrative authority applies for compulsory enforcement;
17. Cases where a maritime arbitration institution applies for preservation measures; cases where, after a ruling has been made by a maritime arbitration institution, one party fails to perform within the prescribed period and the other party applies for enforcement;
18. Other maritime and admiralty cases assigned by a higher people's court or provided by law to be accepted by maritime courts.

IV. The jurisdictional areas of each maritime court are as follows:

1. The Guangzhou Maritime Court has jurisdiction over maritime and admiralty cases occurring within the following area: from the Beilun River Estuary (Dongxing) in the Guangxi Zhuang Autonomous Region in the west, east to the extended sea area at the junction of Guangdong Province and Fujian Province, and the section of water from the Pearl River Estuary to Guangzhou Port, including the South China Sea, Hainan Island, Nan'ao Island, the South China Sea Islands (Dongsha, Xisha, Zhongsha, Nansha, and Huangyan Islands, etc.), and the major ports of Fangcheng, Beihai, Haikou, Sanya, Basuo, Zhanjiang, Huangpu, Guangzhou, Shekou, and Shantou.

2. The Shanghai Maritime Court has jurisdiction over maritime and admiralty cases occurring within the following area: from the junction of Fujian Province and Guangdong Province in the south, north to the extended sea area at the junction of Jiangsu Province and Shandong Province, and the section of water from the Minjiang River Estuary to Fuzhou Port and from the Yangtze River Estuary to Zhangjiagang, including the East China Sea, the southern part of the Yellow Sea, Taiwan Province, offshore islands, and the major ports of Xiamen, Fuzhou, Wenzhou, Ningbo, Shanghai, Nantong, Zhangjiagang, and Lianyungang.

3. The Qingdao Maritime Court has jurisdiction over maritime and admiralty cases occurring within the following area: from the junction of Shandong Province and Jiangsu Province in the south, north to the extended sea area at the junction of Shandong Province and Hebei Province, including part of the Yellow Sea, part of the Bohai Sea, offshore islands, and the major ports of Shijiusuo, Qingdao, Weihai, and Yantai.

4. The Tianjin Maritime Court has jurisdiction over maritime and admiralty cases occurring within the following area: from the junction of Hebei Province and Shandong Province in the south, north to the extended sea area at the junction of Hebei Province and Liaoning Province, including part of the Yellow Sea, part of the Bohai Sea, offshore islands, and the major ports of Tianjin and Qinhuangdao.

5. The Dalian Maritime Court has jurisdiction over maritime and admiralty cases occurring within the following area: from the junction of Liaoning Province and Hebei Province in the south, east to the extended sea area at the Yalu River Estuary and the waters of the Yalu River, including part of the Yellow Sea, part of the Bohai Sea, offshore islands, and the major ports of Dalian and Yingkou.

V. This Decision shall come into force as of the date hereof.
